00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.000
a chance to win.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.909
Believe it or not, babies were the inspiration
behind the movie's menacing clown.

00:00:36.909 --> 00:00:41.451
Yes, right from the start, director Andy Muschietti
knew he wanted his version of Pennywise to

00:00:41.451 --> 00:00:45.660
have a baby-faced appearance with big eyes
and apple cheeks as he felt the contrast of

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.799
the character's child-like features and his
terrifying behaviour would make him all the

00:00:49.799 --> 00:00:50.799
more disturbing.

00:00:50.799 --> 00:00:55.580
Giving Skarsgard his full-on Pennywise face
took around five hours in initial make-up

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:59.830
tests, but they managed to get that down to
around two and a half hours during filming.

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:04.460
That included applying razor-sharp teeth,
prosthetic pieces for his cheeks, and an enlarged

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:10.220
skull which the special effects make-up artists
who crafted it described as a 'gigantic cracked

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:11.220
melon'.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.860
Speaking of teeth, the prosthetics that Skarsgard
wore led to a build-up of drool, so they decided

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:20.500
to embrace that as a feature of his character
and threw in some extra special effects slime

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.990
too for good measure.

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:29.150
To really push the idea of Pennywise's child-like
creepiness even further, the film uses a traditional

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:33.000
English nursery rhyme called Oranges And Lemons
as his musical theme.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.640
The song dates back to at least the 18th-century
and, despite its playful sing-song surface,

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:44.750
its lyrics have dark connotations and may
be linked to public executions or child sacrifice,

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:47.260
making it the perfect theme for Pennywise.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:52.560
When it came to creating Pennywise's peepers,
Skarsgard's blue eyes were turned yellow in

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:53.920
post-production.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.340
But director Andy Muschietti didn't have to
resort to visual effects to produce the wall-eyed

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:03.190
look he wanted for the dancing clown because
Bill Skarsgard has 'a bit of a lazy [left]

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:04.190
eye'.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:08.330
Which means that the Swedish actor was actually
able to make Pennywise's eyes point in different

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:11.090
directions all by himself.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:15.920
As Skarsgard told Dazed magazine, all he had
to do was simply relax the muscle in his left

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.040
eye to make that eye go out and become wall-eyed.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.090
As for Pennywise's clown suit, the director
wanted to avoid what he thought of as the

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.970
cheap look of 20th-century clown costumes.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:31.530
Instead, his instinctive choice was for a
costume that reflected more of a bygone era,

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.870
so to highlight the fact that Pennywise has
been terrorising the inhabitants of Derry,

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:40.260
Maine for an incredibly long time, his suit
combines features from the Middles Ages and

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.780
the Renaissance, as well as Elizabethan and
Victorian periods.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:48.080
And that child-like freakiness of the character
is highlighted once again by what the movie's

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.530
costume designer has called the 'doll-like
quality of his costume'; while the washed-out

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:56.680
colouring of his clothes 'makes him almost
like a shadow'.

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:01.400
While Bill Skarsgard hopes his take on Pennywise
will 'scare a whole generation' just as Tim

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:06.209
Curry did in the 1990 TV mini-series, the
Swedish actor has already experienced the

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:08.450
clown stalking his own sleep.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:13.280
At home in Stockholm after filming had finished,
Skarsgard had recurring visions of Pennywise

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:15.370
in his dreams for two weeks.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:19.681
Speaking to Empire magazine, Skarsgard said
that in the dreams, 'I was [Pennywise], but

00:03:19.681 --> 00:03:21.650
I was in the wrong setting, somehow.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:24.180
I was upset that people could see my face.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:25.180
It was surreal.'

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:30.520
The first time the movie's young stars actually
saw Bill Skarsgard as Pennywise was about

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:35.350
a month into the shoot during a scene they
filmed together where he creeps out of a fridge.

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:39.380
The filmmakers decided to keep Pennywise hidden
away from the young actors until the last

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:43.730
possible moment as they wanted to create a
real feeling of tension so their initial reactions

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:45.980
were as fresh and authentic as possible.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:51.260
For some of the actors who play the pre-teen
members of the Losers' Club, bringing to life

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:56.340
their characters required some extra training,
not in acting, but in how to ride a bike.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:00.170
According to one of the movie's producers,
some of the young leads weren't so hot when

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:04.380
it came to pedal power, so to make sure they
looked like naturals when it came to cycling

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.650
on screen, they were given lessons.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:10.340
In fact, that was just one part of the three
weeks the young cast spent together getting

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:12.330
to know each other before filming began.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:16.699
The idea was for them to establish the kind
of bond they needed to portray on camera so

00:04:16.699 --> 00:04:20.979
they spent lots of time outdoors, swimming,
riding bikes, and playing practical jokes

00:04:20.979 --> 00:04:24.949
on each other, as well as having movie nights
and karaoke sessions.

00:04:24.949 --> 00:04:29.120
On top of that, because the film is set in
the 1980s, one of the producers also made

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:33.939
the young actors an "Intro to the 80s" booklet,
with facts about movies, music, and video

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:36.809
games, as well as how people talked and the
clothes they wore.

00:04:36.809 --> 00:04:41.139
Though I'm guessing that Finn Wolfhard already
knew his 80s stuff thanks to his starring

00:04:41.139 --> 00:04:42.749
role in Stranger Things.

00:04:42.749 --> 00:04:47.939
Fans of Stephen King's novel will likely be
pleased to see the dilapidated house at 29

00:04:47.939 --> 00:04:50.770
Neibolt Street make an appearance in the new
movie.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:54.599
To create the infamous locale, the filmmakers
used two different settings.

00:04:54.599 --> 00:04:59.139
First, they built the exterior of the house
from scratch on a vacant lot in Oshawa, Canada.

00:04:59.139 --> 00:05:04.289
And to get the right aged and run-down appearance,
the design team burned and pressure-washed

00:05:04.289 --> 00:05:06.770
new timber, then painted it silver-grey.

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:10.729
As for that gnarled and twisted tree you can
see in front of the house, that actually started

00:05:10.729 --> 00:05:12.660
out life in a neighbour's garden.

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:16.690
On their way to work one day, a member of
the movie's crew spotted the tree and thought

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:21.490
it looked perfect for filming, so they negotiated
with the tree's owner and nabbed it for the

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:22.490
shoot!

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:26.240
As for the interior of the house, that was
shot in an existing home whose owner gave

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.339
the crew carte blanche to make changes to
creep the place up for filming.

00:05:30.339 --> 00:05:35.119
Those changes included exposing the home's
original Victorian details, coating the walls

00:05:35.119 --> 00:05:40.119
with crumbling plaster, poking vines and dead
leaves through broken windows, covering other

00:05:40.119 --> 00:05:44.819
windows with specially-made Victorian-era
newspapers, and generally filling the place

00:05:44.819 --> 00:05:45.819
with dirt.

